@model PrivacyModel
@inject IConfiguration Configuration
@{
    // ProjectZ's additions to the Core privacy policy (Core only describes what Core itself does).
    // The periods below are code defaults; if the operator overrides them in appsettings, update this page too.
    var qaEnabled = Configuration.GetValue<bool>("Features:QaAssistant:Enabled");
    var qaDays = Configuration.GetValue<int?>("Features:QaAssistant:TurnRetentionDays") ?? 90;
    var telemetryDays = Configuration.GetValue<int?>("Telemetry:RetentionDays") is > 0 and var td ? td : 30;
    var alertWebhook = !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Configuration["Security:AlertWebhookUrl"]);
    var org = Model.Organization ?? "your organization";
}
<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Mobile push notifications</h2>
<p>
    If you allow notifications in the mobile app, the system stores the push subscription provided by your browser
    and delivers notifications through your browser or operating system vendor's push service (for example Google, Apple, or Mozilla);
    notification content is encrypted in transit as required by the Web Push standard.
    You can turn notifications off at any time in your device or browser settings.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Clearing data on mobile sign-out</h2>
<p>When you sign out of the mobile app, the system clears offline data, drafts, and submission records associated with your account from that device.</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Mobile app health reports</h2>
<p>
    To catch problems on shop-floor devices early, the mobile app periodically reports how it is running to this system's server:
    page error messages and details, response times of each function, disconnection periods, the number of operations not yet sent from the device,
    the network type, the mobile app version and the browser identification string (User-Agent),
    together with the signed-in account and plant at the time, and a random identifier stored in the browser to tell devices apart (not a hardware identifier).
    This data stays on <strong>@org</strong>'s server, is used by system administrators for troubleshooting, and is deleted automatically after @telemetryDays days.
</p>

@if (alertWebhook)
{
    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Security alert notifications</h2>
    <p>
        When the system detects a suspected attack (for example a burst of sign-in attempts), it sends the alert type, the source IP address and the time
        to the notification URL configured by the operator so that it can be handled promptly. That URL may belong to an external messaging or monitoring service used by the operator.
    </p>
}

@if (qaEnabled)
{
    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">Data query assistant</h2>
    <p>
        When you use the data query assistant, the system records your user ID, the question text, the interpreted result, and the number of rows returned.
        These records are used to confirm follow-up questions and to improve the query features, and are deleted automatically after @qaDays days.
        Questions are processed by the language model service configured by the operator, which by default runs on the same server as this system;
        they are not sent to external AI services.
    </p>
}

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Automatic deletion</h2>
<p>The system automatically deletes data past its retention period every day (defaults shown; the operator may change them):</p>
<ul>
    <li>Audit logs and approval history: 730 days</li>
    <li>Password history: only the 24 most recent entries per user are kept (hashes only, used to prevent password reuse)</li>
    <li>Scheduled report output files: 90 days</li>
    <li>Material requirements planning (MRP) run records: 180 days</li>
    <li>Mobile app health reports: @telemetryDays days</li>
    @if (qaEnabled)
    {
        <li>Data query assistant records: @qaDays days</li>
    }
</ul>
